  "title": "A colony over a whole session: immortal ants against ants that get old",
  "question": "Same bed, same seed, 500,000 frames. Which one's rise and fall is the one you want in the game?",
  "context_md": "The owner's report this is built against: *the colony grows and breeds until they are so large, they decimate all the plants, the food disappears and then the colony dies.*\n\nBoth panes are the played bed, seed 1, no rain, 500,000 frames at one frame per 12,500. One pane's ants are immortal, which is every animal in the box today. The other's have a 40,000-frame lifespan on the same graded curve the plants die by — half a brood still walking at that age, nineteen in twenty alive at a quarter of it.\n\n**What I already measured, so you do not have to look for it.** The immortal colony swings between 6 and 3,370 ants over the second half of the run and falls from 3,099 to 16 inside one 20,000-frame step. The other holds between 152 and 483 for the last 280,000 frames. Both beds end up poor: neither setting keeps the seed bank up, and that is a different mechanism (what the mouth does to a seed), not this one.\n\n**What to ignore.** Don't judge on how green the bed looks at any one moment — the two arms peak at different times, so at most single instants one looks better purely because it has not crashed *yet*. The question is the shape of the whole run.",
